  intro: "You are going to read an article in which a writer gives her views on 'bucket lists'.",
  textTitle: "Against the bucket list",
  textSub: "Why one writer has no intention of making a list of things to do before she dies",
  text: `At a friend's fiftieth birthday party last spring, the guest of honour stood up after dinner and, to warm applause, read out her bucket list. She was going to swim with dolphins, see the Northern Lights, walk the Inca Trail to Machu Picchu and jump out of an aeroplane, among a dozen other things. Everyone cheered. I cheered too, but on the way home I found myself in an unexpectedly low mood, and it took me some time to work out why. It was not that the list was unambitious; quite the reverse. It was that it could have been written by almost anyone. There was nothing on it, as far as I could tell, that had anything to do with the particular, rather eccentric woman I had known for thirty years.

The phrase 'bucket list' is surprisingly young. It was popularised by a Hollywood film of 2007 in which two terminally ill men set out to do all the things they had always meant to do before they 'kicked the bucket'. The impulse it describes is, of course, much older: people have always dreamt of the things they would do if only they had the time or the money. But the film gave that impulse a name and a shape, and in doing so turned a private longing into something closer to a public project, complete with its own guidebooks, websites and, inevitably, an industry ready to sell the experiences on it.

My first objection is that the bucket list tends to treat experience as a kind of acquisition. Items are not so much enjoyed as completed, and the satisfaction lies at least partly in the crossing off. It is no coincidence that so many of the things on such lists are highly photogenic. The Northern Lights, the summit, the leap from the plane: each yields an image that can be shared, and that serves as proof, to others and perhaps to ourselves, that the thing was actually done. I have watched people at famous viewpoints spend more time arranging the photograph than looking at the view, and I am not sure that they would have made the journey at all if nobody else could see the result.

My second objection is subtler, and perhaps more serious. A list of extraordinary things to do before we die carries with it an implied judgement about the ordinary things we do in the meantime. If life's real meaning is to be found on a glacier or a mountaintop, then the walk to the shops, the conversation over breakfast, the long uneventful evening at home must be something less: a kind of waiting room in which we pass the time until the next item comes up. That seems to me a peculiarly sad way to regard most of one's existence, which will, for nearly all of us, consist of precisely such moments.

I do not want to be unfair. Lists can be a powerful spur to action, and I have seen them work. My aunt, who had been afraid of water all her life, put learning to swim on hers at the age of sixty-eight, and two years later she swam a length of the local pool while her grandchildren cheered from the side. Without the list, she says, she would never have got round to it. I would not dream of arguing with her. But it strikes me that what made her achievement so moving was that it came from her own particular fear and her own particular courage. It was not something she had found in a book of a thousand places to see.

If I were to make a list at all, I think it would look rather different. It would not be a list of things to do before I die, but of things to pay attention to while I am alive: the light on the river in the late afternoon, the sound of my children talking in the next room, the first cup of coffee of the day. None of these would sound very impressive read out after dinner, and none of them can ever be crossed off. That, I suspect, is rather the point.`,
